// A `#run` inside a procedure **body** (ADR-0069 §2) — the other half of W4's first sub-wave.
//
// Before this, `n := #run add(2, 3);` refused with "`#run` has no value until jr-vm (ADR-0016 §4)":
// only *file-level* constants were evaluated, so a body `#run` had no value to lower to. Now
// `file_consts` collects them too, in the same round-robin and behind the same cycle detector — because
// two places evaluating `#run` would be two chances to disagree about what a `#run` means.
//
// Six things here are worth checking rather than assuming, and the `exit` gives each teeth:
//
//   * a body `#run` **computes at compile time** and the body contains the result — it is not a call the
//     body performs (ADR-0069 §2 rejects that reading explicitly);
//   * a body's expressions live in **its own arena**, which starts at index 0 exactly as the file's
//     does — so every map lookup had to learn the scope. Reading the wrong arena finds a *different
//     expression* rather than failing, which is why the thunk carries the scope rather than assuming
//     file level;
//   * a `#run` whose callee **loops** works, which it already did at file scope;
//   * a `#run` may appear **more than once** in one body, and each gets its own value;
//   * a `#run` in a body and one at file scope coexist, evaluated by the same query;
//   * both engines agree, because both receive the same constant — there is no comptime call left in the
//     MIR for them to disagree about.
//
// What a body `#run` may *not* do is exactly what a file-scope one may not do (ADR-0069 §3): a
// `#foreign` call is refused by ADR-0006, and a call that omits a defaulted argument is refused because
// const-eval runs before the check phase that fills it. The position changed; the rules did not.
#import "Basic";

/// Plain arithmetic, for the simplest body `#run`.
add :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> s64 {
    return a + b;
}

/// A **loop** in the callee, so the body case covers what the file-scope case already did: the
/// interpreter runs real control flow, not just folded arithmetic.
sum_to :: (n: s64) -> s64 {
    t := 0;
    i := 0;
    while i < n {
        t = t + i;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return t;
}

/// A file-scope `#run` in the same file, so both kinds are evaluated by one query in one round.
FILE_LEVEL :: #run add(10, 20);

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // The simplest case: a value computed while compiling.
    a := #run add(2, 3);
    if a == 5 {
        n = n + 1;
    }

    // A second `#run` in the same body gets its own value — they are keyed by expression, so two in one
    // body cannot collide.
    b := #run add(4, 4);
    if b == 8 {
        n = n + 2;
    }

    // A loop in the callee.
    c := #run sum_to(5);
    if c == 10 {
        n = n + 4;
    }

    // Nested calls and arithmetic around the call, both of which already worked at file scope — checked
    // here because the *position* is what changed, and a claim about one position is not one about both.
    d := #run add(add(1, 2), 3) * 2;
    if d == 12 {
        n = n + 8;
    }

    // The file-scope constant, so this file proves the two kinds coexist.
    if FILE_LEVEL == 30 {
        n = n + 16;
    }

    // Every assertion: 31.
    if n == 31 {
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}
